          {
            "url": "https://cambridgeglobalist.org/2019/11/19/us-and-china-reach-phase-one-trade-deal/",
            "title": "US and China reach 'Phase One' trade deal - Cambridge Globalist",
            "snippets": [
              "The US and China have tentatively agreed to a ‘Phase One’ trade deal, providing a ceasefire in ongoing tariff battles and trade talks which have been going on since early 2018. China has ultimately been aiming for a rollback on tariffs imposed on $112 billion of Chinese goods and a decrease in the 25% levies...",
              "# US and China reach ‘Phase One’ trade deal\nThe US and China have tentatively agreed to a ‘Phase One’ trade deal, providing a ceasefire in ongoing tariff battles and trade talks which have been going on since early 2018. China has ultimately been aiming for a rollback on tariffs imposed on $112 billion of Chinese goods and a decrease in the 25% levies imposed on $250 billion of Chinese goods by the US since the trade war began last year. It is possible that additional tariffs may be removed in future phases",
              "China has claimed that the US has agreed to roll back these tariffs, but Trump hit back soon after, claiming that they have actually not yet agreed on whether tariffs will be removed at all. The development of this dispute has been influenced by a multitude of factors, including geopolitical rivalry, contradictory messages from Trump which have impeded negotiations, tensions over the South China Sea and a growing trend towards protectionist economic policy",
              "Fractured US-China relations have created volatility in the global financial system. There has been a mass sale of global assets by Chinese companies, and Chinese companies have become net sellers of global assets for the first time in ten years. American companies are central to the profile of those being sold, suggesting that the trade dispute is implicated in this trend. There has also been a more general fall in business confidence due to uncertainty. US consumers and importers and Chinese exporters are paying for the tariffs through higher prices on goods. Trade tensions have also triggered fluctuations in China’s currency",
              "The trade war was likely the most significant cause of the renminbi – the official currency of the People’s Republic of China – weakening to its lowest level since the global financial crisis. It has now strengthened past seven to the dollar, perhaps due to the US potentially rolling back some tariffs on Chinese goods in the ‘Phase One’ agreement",
              "To exacerbate this, the US has experienced a recent and unexpected fall in productivity, which the trade war likely plays into as it disrupts global supply chains. US companies have also begun to show caution in capital expenditure. Trump should perhaps take these factors into account in light of the upcoming US 2020 presidential elections and seek to find a resolution with haste",
              "China has not been passive in these trade discussions, though. The WTO recently permitted China to take unprecedented countermeasures against the US, and so it now may impose $3.6bn in levies on American goods in retaliation against actions from Washington. These levies are still small in comparison to the US tariffs imposed on China, but they do serve to highlight the considerably aggressive nature of US-China relations",
              "The ‘Phase One’ limited trade deal covers some key tensions such as reduced tariffs on Chinese agricultural goods and commodities, in return for concessions from China such as greater protection for intellectual property for US companies. However, this deal may not necessarily roll back existing tariffs, merely hold off on the creation of new ones. It might be questioned, therefore, if this deal is somewhat superficial. Both sides have significant incentives to make a deal that works, but there is also the matter of neither side wanting to appear weak",
              "This attempt to deescalate tensions does suggest that both the US and China want to create a more durable and encompassing deal than indicated by previous talks",

